MUTATION CONTROLS
================================================================================================================
  a0 / 10  : M(<80) = 2.045e11 vs 4.977e11 (smaller a0 = larger y = the kernel switched OFF; note a0 x 10 does
             NOT switch it off in an EFE-dominated regime -- it saturates at nu(e), which is the correct physics)
  nu = 1   : M(<80) = 0.666e11 (baryons only, a factor 7.5 below the prediction)
  [PASS] MUT the kernel carries the prediction: a0/10 and nu = 1 both collapse it toward the baryon mass
         (framework 4.98e11, a0/10 2.04e11, baryons only 0.67e11)

================================================================================================================
READING
================================================================================================================
  Written from the numbers above, after they printed.

  1. THE HACK COST MORE THAN THE PHYSICS DID.  The scalar-additive external field DW1 used is the
     maximum-suppression case; the exact angle average is +16 per cent higher at 80 kpc
     (canonical) and +13 per cent (alt).  DW1's -4.11 sigma becomes -2.77 and its
     -3.70 becomes -2.50.  The EFE tension at 80 kpc is REAL BUT MODEST, and the framework sits
     inside 3 sigma on both footings once the vector average is done properly.

  2. AND THE SHAPE SURVIVES -- DW1's 'the EFE spoils the shape' IS RETRACTED.  With the proper EFE the
     13-point profile fits at chi2/dof 0.38 (canonical) / 0.49 (alt), rms 3.3 / 4.0 per cent,
     against 7.0 / 7.8 per cent for the scalar-additive version.  The degradation was the approximation,
     not the data: the shape prefers a weak field only because the HACK was strong.

  3. THE AMPLITUDE MOVES UP WITH THE FIELD, AS IT MUST, AND THE TWO-DATASET AGREEMENT SURVIVES:
     A* = 1.360 canonical / 1.205 alt at e_N = 0.01273, against 1.325 / 1.145 at zero field,
     and still within 3 sigma of the outer rotation curve's own demand (item 35: 1.254-1.309 / 1.152-1.208).
     The cost is a larger baryon excess: 21-36 per cent over the census, which
     remains the framework's one free ledge and its most falsifiable number.

  4. THE PLATEAU -- THE NUMBER ROW 32 TESTS -- IS LOWER THAN THE NAIVE CAP.  At e_N = 0.01273:
     M(<200 kpc) = 5.26e11 canonical / 5.27e11 alt at A = 1, and 7.16 / 6.35
     at the fitted amplitude, against the paper's own NFW extrapolation 11.35e11 (a 1.6x
     to 2.2x split).  Note it is BELOW the nu(e_N) M_b = 6.27e11 that item 38 used as the cap: the
     angle average of nu over the field's own anisotropy costs a further 16 per cent, so the EFE cap was
     itself an overestimate.  The registered kill (M(<200 kpc) >= 1.0e12) is unaffected -- at the RC-preferred
     weak field e_N = 0.008 the plateau is 6.54e11 and still below it.

  5. THE ANISOTROPY IS THE REAL CAVEAT, AND IT IS NOT SMALL AT LARGE RADIUS.  The angle average converges
     (1.06 from 80 to 150 kpc) while the PARALLEL direction diverges (2.7x):
     along the field the external acceleration itself is credited to the Galaxy.  A spherical Jeans
     analysis is therefore unbiased only for an ANGLE-ISOTROPIC tracer sample; at 80 kpc the parallel and
     perpendicular readings differ by 58 and 1 per cent.  That is a test for DR4
     (split the halo tracers by angle to the Local Group's field), and it is the same azimuthal channel
     hunt item 81 priced at 0.3-1.4 per cent on the rotation curve.

  6. HONEST LIMITS.  This is QUMOND (the algebraic kernel); h34's own AQUAL solve finds the algebraic boost
     ~15 per cent HIGH, so the EFE suppression computed here is, if anything, understated and the plateau
     correspondingly overstated.  The external field is uniform and static, and the LMC's contribution to it
     is neglected.  The amplitude A remains a nuisance, not a derivation.  kappa = 1/2 stays FITTED.

